class.description:
Every verb takes the value and answers the value, so a description written once runs in all three [enum Mode] values: [method writer] spends bits, [method reader] takes them back, and [method measurer] counts what they would cost without storing anything. Write the description as one function over a [NetwBitStream] and call it three times rather than writing an encoder and a decoder that must be kept in step.
[codeblock]
func describe(s: NetwBitStream, row: Dictionary) -> Dictionary:
    row.hp = s.bits(row.get("hp", 0), 12)
    row.name = s.string(row.get("name", ""))
    s.align_verify()
    return row

var out := NetwBitStream.writer()
describe(out, {"hp": 87, "name": "hero"})
var bytes := out.to_bytes()

var back := NetwBitStream.reader(bytes)
var row := describe(back, {})
if not back.ok() or back.bits_remaining() != 0:
    push_error("refused")
[/codeblock]
[b]A failed read poisons the stream.[/b] Every verb after the failure is a no-op answering the value it was given, so a decoder checks [method ok] once at the end rather than after each call. This is what makes a hand-written decoder safe against a remote sender: the values a poisoned read answers are the caller's own defaults and never bytes that were not there.
[b]A decoder that stops before its input is exhausted has not decoded.[/b] [method bits_remaining] reading anything but [code]0[/code] after a whole payload means the sender framed it under a description this one does not share, so refuse it rather than keep the part that parsed.
[method align_verify] pads to the next byte on a write and, on a read, refuses padding that is not zero. A payload ends with it so its length is a whole number of bytes and its tail cannot carry anything undeclared.
[method varuint] and [method svarint] are canonical and bounded by the [code]max_bytes[/code] the field declares: a value too large to spell in that many groups is refused rather than truncated, and a redundant final group is refused rather than accepted as a second spelling of one number.
This is the same vocabulary the framework's own frames are written in, and [code]extension/WIRE.md[/code] states them field for field in it.
